\title{Multiplicity in Pascal's Triangle:\\
Smoothness Hierarchies, Density, Sharp Thresholds,\\
and the Complete Classification of Adjacent Repetitions}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{9 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
For an integer $t \ge 2$ let $N(t) = \#\{(n,k) : 0 \le k \le n,\ \binom{n}{k} = t\}$ be the
\emph{multiplicity} of $t$ in Pascal's triangle. Singmaster's conjecture (1971) asserts that
$N$ is bounded; it remains open. We prove a \emph{smoothness hierarchy}: if $N(t) \ge 2m+2$
then every prime factor $p$ of $t$ satisfies $\binom{p}{m+1} \le t$, hence
$(p-m)^{m+1} \le (m+1)!\,t$. The base case states that $N(t) \ge 3$ forces $p(p-1) \le 2t$
for every prime $p \mid t$; contrapositively, any $t$ with a prime factor exceeding
$\sqrt{2t}+1$ has $N(t) = 2$ exactly, whence $N(cp) = 2$ for every $c \ge 1$ and every prime
$p > 2c+1$. We prove a counting bound
$\#\{t \le X : N(t) \ge 3\} \le (\sqrt{2X}+2)(\log_2 X + 1)$, so multiplicity exactly two has
density one; and we sharpen the classical elementary bound $N(t) \le 2\log_2 t$ by a factor of
two, to $N(t) \le \log_2 t + \log_2(2\log_2 t+1) + 1$. We determine the sharp thresholds: the
least $t$ with $N(t) \ge 3, 4, 6, 8$ is $6, 10, 120, 3003$. Finally we completely classify
\emph{adjacent repetitions} $\binom{n}{k} = \binom{n-1}{k+1}$: clearing factorials, completing
the square, and descending on the norm form $x^2-xy-y^2 = \pm 5$ shows all solutions are
consecutive Lucas pairs, and a Lucas--Fibonacci dictionary driven by Cassini's identity
identifies them with Singmaster's Fibonacci family. Hence the adjacent repetitions are exactly
$(n,k) = (F_{2i+4}F_{2i+5},\, F_{2i+2}F_{2i+5})$, $i \ge 0$, namely
$(15,5), (104,39), (714,272), (4895,1869), \dots$, and $3003$ is the only value below $10^6$
arising this way.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Pascal's triangle, binomial coefficients, Singmaster's conjecture,
multiplicity, smooth numbers, Fibonacci numbers, Lucas numbers, Cassini's identity,
Diophantine descent.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

Pascal's triangle contains every integer $t \ge 2$ at least twice, since
$\binom{t}{1} = \binom{t}{t-1} = t$. Empirically, almost every integer appears exactly twice,
but exceptions occur.

